  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Question": "One interior angle of an isosceles triangle is \\(100^\\circ\\). How is this triangle classified by its angles?",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "Right triangle",
    "B": "Acute triangle",
    "C": "Equilateral triangle",
    "D": "Obtuse triangle"
  },
  "Correct Answer": "D",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "\\(100^\\circ\\) is an obtuse angle and can only be the vertex angle (the two base angles are equal; if a base angle were \\(100^\\circ\\), their sum would be \\(200^\\circ>180^\\circ\\)). The other two angles are each \\(40^\\circ\\). Since it has an obtuse angle, it is an obtuse triangle.",
